{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791372951,"nonce":"ggZg3fqAhrh4JWz1vJ1HxGxrW2w=","message":"The aggregate type comparison was corrected, but that alone did not remove the dictionary stack copies. The actual remaining ordering gap was aggregate_split running before late box-reader expansion and dead-predecessor cleanup exposed same-block field reads. Re-running aggregate splitting after that cleanup restores direct field reads. A pipeline regression covers an aggregate load and extracts separated by a block with a dead predecessor.\n\nNUC native-code comparison of tokenizer at baseline vs v5: all generated functions match after normalizing host addresses except zl_meta_of, where an empty jump block disappears (309 -\u003e 307 VCode lines). The tokenizer main and dictionary lookup helpers now match baseline, but the between-build timing difference persists. Earlier stack-copy attribution was a hypothesis, not a complete explanation of the measured slowdown. Runtime-only range-switch controls do not change the builtins snapshot baked at build time.\n\nAdded conservative store-width handling for aggregate values carried as pointers: a logical aggregate store cannot be treated as an 8-byte pointer write in the new byte-range proof. Five integration tests now pass locally and on NUC, including the three-tier overlap/alias execution test; seven load-CSE unit tests pass on NUC. Final full LLVM-off benchmark sweep and LLVM-off/on conformance checks are underway. No commit or issue closure yet.","files":null}]}